ok let's get into audience questions for  the week first question today hey David  could the United States ever actually  become a truly socialist or communist  country this is a hard question to even  dig into because it depends on what you  mean with those terms ok so first of all  there aren't really 100% unanimously  agreed upon definitions of those systems  but we can sort of think about it in the  following way  socialism is a system where workers own  the means of production so you don't  have private ownership of businesses and  in many cases you don't have private  ownership of property although sometimes  you can have the private property but  you don't have the private businesses  where all employees own the businesses  but individuals can still own private  property you have other situations where  the government owns all the companies  but that sometimes is called communism  if it's still a profit-driven market  economy but the government owns all the  companies that's not really communism  that's more actually state capitalism  then you get to when the government owns  the means of production in a non  free-market economy private property is  often also eliminated altogether that's  the closest to the style of the Marxist  Leninist or Stalinist Leninist  ideal and that is sort of like a working  definition of what communism might be  but the key question here is could this  happen in the United States I believe  that full-on socialism and full-on  capitalism are not going to happen in  the United States if ever for a very  very long time and the reason I say that  is right now first of all the United  States is not a fully capitalist country  to begin with right the US has never  been a totally capitalist system to  begin with a real lazy fair system as it  would be called government has been  intertwined with the economy subsidies  taxes education Medicare the postal  service contracts for private companies  from the government there's this endless  list where this has never actually been  a true lays affair totally capitalist  market driven system and in that same  sense the idea that we would ever fully  become a socialist or communist nation  is unlikely from a merely structural  perspective because most countries in  the world are not uniquely just one  system they're sort of blends in some  ways the other reason why at least  anytime in the near future  total socialism or total communism isn't  likely in the United States is that  there's no major socialist or communist  party in the United States yes over the  last ten years we've seen the word  socialism have fewer and fewer negative  connotations compared to where it was  coming out of the Cold War for example  but the most far left Democrats are  basically Social Democrats there's a  handful of actual Democratic socialists  many call themselves Democratic  Socialist without actually being  socialists so there's not even a  significant clamoring for socialism in  the United States if you believe Marxist  theory then you could conceive of the  idea that there will eventually be a  sort of uprising of the proletariat  against the bourgeoisie and that you  will have a Marxist uh pending of our  system but short of believing that Marx  will be fulfilled to the letter at some  point in the future we are more than  likely going to continue indefinitely  having a mixed system a system that has  elements of capitalism and social  democracy and even maybe some elements  of democratic socialism but to truly get  to one of those systems you would need  one a a real desire for that or if the  idea is a dictator will appear from  above and impose that you would need to  first eliminate a lot of the sort of  constitutional and democratic safeguards  that exist which would allow such a  system to come to power so I don't see  it as I've said before I believe that  the best  are mixed full-fledged capitalism is a  disaster Stalinist Leninism is a  disaster authoritarian police states are  bad I believe that very well regulated  capitalism with a significant floor so  that people don't fall below and  significant spending on socially  democratic programs is the way forward
 

